# PDF & Document Extraction

For DOCX: use `python-docx` (parses actual document structure, far better than OCR).
For PPTX: see the `powerpoint` skill (uses `python-pptx` with full slide/notes support).
This skill covers **PDFs and scanned documents**.

## Step 1: Remote URL Available?

If the document has a URL, **always try `web_extract` first**:

```
web_extract(urls=["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.03300"])
web_extract(urls=["https://example.com/report.pdf"])
```

This handles PDF-to-markdown conversion via Firecrawl with no local dependencies.

Only use local extraction when: the file is local, web_extract fails, or you need batch processing.

## Step 2: Choose Local Extractor

| Feature | pymupdf (~25MB) | marker-pdf (~3-5GB) | tesseract (~10-100MB) |
|---------|-----------------|---------------------|-----------------------|
| **Text-based PDF** | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ (image-only) |
| **Scanned PDF (OCR)** | ❌ | ✅ (90+ languages) | ✅ (100+ languages) |
| **Multi-language OCR** | ❌ | ✅ (per-page language mix harder) | ✅ (combine languages: `-l vie+fra+eng`) |
| **Tables** | ✅ (basic) | ✅ (high accuracy) | ❌ |
| **Equations / LaTeX** | ❌ | ✅ | ❌ |
| **Code blocks** | ❌ | ✅ | ❌ |
| **Forms** | ❌ | ✅ | ❌ |
| **Headers/footers removal** | ❌ | ✅ | ❌ |
| **Reading order detection** | ❌ | ✅ | ❌ |
| **Images extraction** | ✅ (embedded) | ✅ (with context) | ❌ |
| **Install size** | ~25MB | ~3-5GB (PyTorch + models) | ~10-100MB (per language pack) |
| **Speed** | Instant | ~1-14s/page (CPU) | ~2-10s/page (CPU) |
| **Best for** | Native digital PDFs | Complex layouts, high-quality OCR | Quick multilingual OCR on scanned texts |

**Decision**: Use pymupdf unless you need OCR, equations, forms, or complex layout analysis.

If the user needs marker capabilities but the system lacks ~5GB free disk:
> "This document needs OCR/advanced extraction (marker-pdf), which requires ~5GB for PyTorch and models. Your system has [X]GB free. Options: free up space, provide a URL so I can use web_extract, or I can try pymupdf which works for text-based PDFs but not scanned documents or equations."

---

**Note for multilingual French/Vietnamese documents**: Use `tesseract` with combined language packs:
```bash
# Install dependencies
apt-get install -y poppler-utils tesseract-ocr
# Add language packs
apt-get install -y tesseract-ocr-vie tesseract-ocr-fra tesseract-ocr-eng
# Check installed languages
tesseract --list-langs

# Convert PDF page to image, then OCR (works on ANY scanned PDF regardless of layout complexity)
pdftoppm -f 1 -l 1 -r 300 document.pdf /tmp/page
tesseract /tmp/page-001.ppm stdout -l vie+fra+eng --psm 1

# Batch OCR all pages of a scanned PDF
# Use a script (see references for the batch script)
```

**When to use tesseract over marker-pdf:**
- You only need text (not layout, tables, or formatting)
- The document is in Vietnamese, French, or another language marker-pdf doesn't handle well
- You don't have ~5GB free for marker-pdf's PyTorch models
- You need to combine multiple languages on the same page (`-l vie+fra+eng`)

**When NOT to use tesseract:**
- The PDF has complex layout (columns, tables, sidebars) — use marker-pdf
- The PDF has embedded text (use pymupdf — instant and free)
- The document is handwritten — use `vision_analyze`

**Output is raw text** — no markdown, no formatting preservation. Post-processing may be needed for:
- Vietnamese diacritic cleanup (tesseract sometimes drops or misplaces tone marks)
- Hyphenation repair (common in multi-column scanned books)
- French loanword reconstruction (Vietnamese tesseract models may treat French terms as noise)

See `references/vietnamese-ocr-cleaning.md` for post-processing patterns specific to Vietnamese historical texts.

### Batch OCR — Disk & Temp File Management

Tesseract batch jobs on scanned books produce huge intermediate files — each page at 300 DPI is ~25MB as a PPM image. A 500-page volume = ~12.5GB of temp files. **Without guaranteed cleanup, a single interrupted run can fill /tmp and crash every service on the host.**

**Mandatory pattern — `trap` for guaranteed cleanup:**
```bash
TMPDIR=$(mktemp -d /tmp/ocr_XXXXX)
trap 'rm -rf "$TMPDIR"' EXIT
cd "$TMPDIR"

pdftoppm -r 300 /path/to/input.pdf ./page
for f in page-*.ppm; do
  tesseract "$f" "${f%.ppm}" -l vie+fra --psm 1 2>/dev/null
done
# ... concatenate output ...
mv output.txt /path/to/final.txt
# trap fires on success, error, SIGTERM, or crash
```

**Key rules:**
- **Always** `trap cleanup EXIT` before creating temp dirs. The `rm -rf` at the end of a script is not enough — `set -e`, SIGTERM, or a crash mid-way skips it.
- **Check disk before starting** — run `df -h /` first. /tmp may not be tmpfs (on this VPS it's on the root partition).
- **Check RAM before starting** — run `free -h`. Tesseract with 2+ language models (e.g. `-l vie+fra`) uses 200-400MB per invocation. On a 2GB VPS, running tesseract alongside system services (gateway ~230MB, gbrain ~130MB, Hermes workers ~200MB) can trigger OOM kills, even with a single tesseract process. If `available` < 1GB, wait for other tasks to finish or reduce DPI to 150.
- **Estimate space**: pages × ~25MB ÷ 1024 = GB needed at 300 DPI. At 200 DPI it's ~12MB per page. At 150 DPI it's ~5MB. Add 20% buffer.
- **Run multi-volume batches SEQUENTIALLY, not in parallel.** Each volume needs ~9-12GB temp space (727 pages × 12-25MB/page). Running 5+ volumes concurrently on a 38GB disk guarantees disk-full failures. One at a time lets each finish, trap-cleanup, free space, then start the next.
- **Watch the 30-min timeout.** Background processes with `notify_on_complete=true` have a 30-min timeout by default (1800s). A 500-page OCR at 6s/page takes exactly 50 min. Set `timeout=3600` explicitly for long scripts, or chain them: run one volume per invocation and let the completion notification trigger the next.
- **Don't use fixed temp dir names** — use `mktemp -d` so concurrent runs don't collide.
- **`set -e` is dangerous in disk-constrained OCR loops.** If pdftoppm fails (ENOSPC, corrupt PDF page, transient IO error), `set -e` kills the entire script — including your trap cleanup if the failure is a SIGKILL. Always guard single-page conversions with `if pdftoppm ...; then` rather than relying on `set -e` to catch nothing:
  ```bash
  # BAD — set -e kills the loop on any pdftoppm failure:
  for ((P=1; P<=TOTAL; P++)); do
      pdftoppm -r 200 -f "$P" -l "$P" "$PDF" "$TMPDIR/p"
      tesseract "$TMPDIR/p-$P.ppm" stdout -l vie+fra >> "$OUT"
  done

  # GOOD — pdftoppm failure skips that page, loop continues:
  for ((P=1; P<=TOTAL; P++)); do
      if pdftoppm -r 200 -f "$P" -l "$P" "$PDF" "$TMPDIR/p" 2>/dev/null; then
          tesseract "$TMPDIR/p-$P.ppm" stdout -l vie+fra >> "$OUT"
          rm -f "$TMPDIR/p-$P.ppm"
      else
          echo "=== PAGE $P (CONVERSION FAILED) === " >> "$OUT"
      fi
  done
  ```
  This way a corrupt page, disk hiccup, or transient poppler failure logs a skipped page marker instead of aborting the entire volume. The output file shows exactly which pages need reprocessing.
- **Page-at-a-time vs. batch conversion:** `pdftoppm -r 300 input.pdf ./page` (batch) converts all pages at once, needing ~12GB for a 500-page volume. `pdftoppm -r 200 -f $P -l $P` (one page) uses <25MB at a time at 200 DPI. For constrained systems, always prefer page-at-a-time with single-image cleanup after tesseract.
- **Multi-volume projects**: Create kanban cards per volume for visibility. Never run in parallel on constrained systems.
- **⚠️ Resume-by-last-page gap failure**: The canonical resume pattern (`LAST=$(grep ... | tail -1)`) fails when the output has gaps — pages that were never processed but a later page marker exists. The script exits saying "already complete" while pages are missing. Always check for gaps before trusting resume: `grep "^=== PAGE" "$OUT" | sed 's/=== PAGE //;s/ ===//' | sort -n | awk 'NR>1{for(i=prev+1;i<$1;i++) print "GAP:", i} {prev=$1}'`. See `references/batch-ocr-pipeline.md` → "Resume-by-last-page failure with gaps" for full detection, the sed truncation fix, and `at now` persistence for long runs.
- **Disk check**: Always `df -h /` before starting batch OCR. This VPS has 38GB root — one volume at ~9GB temp can fill it if other services are running.

```bash
pip install pymupdf pymupdf4llm
```

**Via helper script**:
```bash
python scripts/extract_pymupdf.py document.pdf              # Plain text
python scripts/extract_pymupdf.py document.pdf --markdown    # Markdown
python scripts/extract_pymupdf.py document.pdf --tables      # Tables
python scripts/extract_pymupdf.py document.pdf --images out/ # Extract images
python scripts/extract_pymupdf.py document.pdf --metadata    # Title, author, pages
python scripts/extract_pymupdf.py document.pdf --pages 0-4   # Specific pages
```

**Inline**:
```bash
python3 -c "
import pymupdf
doc = pymupdf.open('document.pdf')
for page in doc:
    print(page.get_text())
"
```

